WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.304
Checks and balances on the choice itself.

2
00:00:02.584 --> 00:00:10.541
A model choice is a claim about the future (“this model will keep handling our traffic well”), so it needs the same checks as any other claim.

3
00:00:10.821 --> 00:00:11.867
Pin and date it.

4
00:00:12.147 --> 00:00:16.541
Record the exact model id, the effort setting, and the date of the eval.

5
00:00:16.821 --> 00:00:22.389
Current Claude model ids are pinned snapshots; other providers offer pinned versions too.

6
00:00:22.669 --> 00:00:26.765
An alias that silently moves to a new model invalidates your eval.

7
00:00:27.045 --> 00:00:28.283
Keep the eval set.

8
00:00:28.563 --> 00:00:35.176
When a new model ships, rerunning the same set is an afternoon; without it, you are back to leaderboards and opinions.

9
00:00:35.456 --> 00:00:37.568
Watch production, not just the eval.

10
00:00:37.848 --> 00:00:42.989
Sample live outputs for review and track the failure categories you found during the eval.

11
00:00:43.269 --> 00:00:47.472
Drift in your inputs can break a model choice as surely as a model change.

12
00:00:47.752 --> 00:00:48.861
Plan the fallback.

13
00:00:49.141 --> 00:00:55.733
If your chosen provider has an outage or changes terms, which model is second, and has it passed the same eval?

14
00:00:56.013 --> 00:01:00.643
Studio 6 covers routing and fallbacks; the playbook should name the second choice.

15
00:01:00.923 --> 00:01:02.949
Separate “best model” from “best system.

16
00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:07.603
” Many quality problems are prompt, context, or retrieval problems.

17
00:01:07.883 --> 00:01:12.043
If no model passes, fix the system before shopping for a bigger model.
